  <page>

    <exercise>
        <title>Spacetime Physics 8-3</title>
        <p>
            A few notes to help you with each of the parts:
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Part a) is like a variable mass problem: if you gain momentum <m>d\vec{p}</m>
                        in time <m>dt</m>, the force acting on you is <m>\vec{f}=\dfrac{d\vec{p}}{dt}</m>.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        As explained in sample problem 8-5, to go from intensity (power per area) to absorbed
                        power, you use the cross section <m>\pi r^2</m> of the satellite.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        You may use, without explanation, that the intensity of solar radiation at distance <m>R</m>
                        from the sun is <m>I(R)=I_*  \dfrac{R_E^2}{R^2}</m> , where <m>I_*</m> is the solar constant
                        and <m>R_E</m> is the Earth-sun distance (150 million km). 
                        The quick explanation is that the solar power output is a constant <m>P_0</m>, and that power
                        radiates in every direction, so the intensity (power/area) at any distance is 
                        <m>P_0/4πR^2</m>. You'll also need the idea from b/c.

                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </p>
        
    </exercise>
