    <exercise>
        <title>Impossible Processes</title>
        <p>
            Show that the following processes are dynamically impossible. Lots of ways of approaching these: 
            you can try brute-forcing the conservation laws, seeing things in a clever reference-frame, 
            using an elegant 4-momentum argument (or some combination of all three!).
        </p>
        <p>
            <ol>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        A single photon strikes a stationary electron and gives up all its energy to the electron.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        A single photon in empty space is transformed into an electron and a positron.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        A fast positron and a stationary electron annihilate, producing only one photon.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ol>
        </p>
    </exercise>
    
    <exercise>
        <title>Uranium Fission</title>
        <p>
            	A common fission process for uranium is 
                
                <md>n+\ce{^{235}_{92}U}→\ce{_{36}^{141}Ba}+\ce{_{56}^{92}Kr}+3n</md>. 
                
                The masses of neutron, uranium, barium, and krypton are, respectively, 1.0086u, 234.9934673u, 
                140.8836846u, and 91.906425u.
                <ol>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            How much energy (in MeV) is generated in this process? 1u<m>c^2</m>=931.5MeV
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                          	As a fraction of the initial mass, how much mass is lost in this process?
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                             How much energy can be generated in this way from 1kg of Uranium? 
                             Compare with the energy generated by burning 1kg of fossil fuels, 
                             which is roughly <m>50\times 10^6</m> J
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
        </p>
        
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
        <title>Practicing Taylor Expansions</title>
        <p>
            You shoot a pion and a muon, both with an energy of 10 GeV, down a 1km tunnel. By how much distance 
            does the muon win? The pion has a mass of 134 MeV, and the muon has a mass of 105 MeV. So note that the 
            gamma factors are quite large (roughly 100 for the muon). Do this in two steps:
        </p>
        <p>
            <ol>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        First, using the binomial expansion <m>(1+x)^n\simeq 1+nx</m>, 
                        valid when <m>nx\ll 1</m>, derive an expression for <m>\beta</m> as a function of 
                        <m>\gamma</m>, which for you will be valid when <m>\gamma \gg 1</m>.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Now use basic kinematics, as well as any other approximations you see fit, to find out by 
                        how much the muon comes out ahead. The lesson here is: series expansions are your friend. 
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ol>
        </p>
    </exercise>
  </page>
</worksheet>